WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:07.700
We've talked about how you want your learning algorithm to do well on the training set,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.820
but sometimes you don't actually want to do too well.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.540
And knowing what human level performance is can tell you exactly how well but not too

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.780
well you want your algorithm to do on the training set.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.700
Let me show you what I mean.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.940
We've used CAD classification a lot and given a picture, let's say humans have near perfect

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.940
accuracy.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.300
So the human level error is 1%.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:39.620
In that case, if your learning algorithm achieves 8% training error and 10% depth error, then

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:44.620
maybe you wanted to do better on the training set.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.900
So the fact that there's a huge gap between how well your algorithm does on your training

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.660
set versus how well humans do shows that your algorithm isn't even fitting the training set

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:55.820
well.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:02.260
So in terms of tools to reduce bias or variance, in this case, I would say focus on a very

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.980
reducing bias.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.500
So you want to do things like find a train a bigger neural network or run greater than

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.700
10% longer.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.620
Just try to do better on the training set.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.500
But now let's look at the same training error and depth error and imagine that human level

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.300
performance was not 1%.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.820
So we just copied this over.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.660
But in a different application or maybe on a different data set, let's say that human level

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.480
error is actually 7.5%.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.880
Maybe the images in your data set are so blurry that even humans can't tell whether there's

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.960
a cat in this picture.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.280
This example is maybe slightly contrived because humans are actually very good at looking at

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.800
pictures and telling if there's a cat in it or not.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.600
But for the sake of this example, let's say your data sets images are so blurry or

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.960
so low resolution that even humans get 7.5% error.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.360
In this case, even though your training error and depth error are the same as the earlier

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.880
example, you see that maybe you're actually doing just fine on the training set.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.240
It's doing only a little bit worse than human level performance.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.640
And in this second example, you would maybe want to focus on reducing this component,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.560
reducing the variance in your learning algorithm.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.880
So you might try regularization to try to bring your depth error closer to your training

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.800
error, for example.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:32.000
So in the earlier course's discussion on bias and variance, we were mainly assuming that

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.020
there were tasks where Bayes' error is nearly zero.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:44.060
So to explain what just happened here, for our cat classification example, think of human

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:56.620
level error as a proxy or as an estimate for Bayes' error, for Bayes' optimal error.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.860
And for computer vision tasks, this is a pretty reasonable proxy because humans are actually

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.900
very good at computer vision.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.980
And so whatever a human can do is maybe not too far from Bayes' error.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:14.380
By definition, human level error is worse than Bayes' error because nothing can be

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.980
better than Bayes' error, but human level error might not be too far from Bayes'

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.160
error.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.340
So the surprising thing we saw here is that depending on what human level error is,

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:31.460
or really this is really an approximately Bayes' error, or so we assume it to be, but

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:39.860
depending on what we think is achievable, with the same training error and depth error,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:45.700
in these two cases we decided to focus on bias reduction tactics or on variance reduction

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.340
tactics.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:54.320
And what happened is in the example on the left, 8% training error is really high when

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.180
you think you could get it down to 1%, and so bias reduction tactics could help you do

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.180
that.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:07.780
Whereas in the example on the right, if you think that Bayes' error is 7.5%, and here

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.780
we're using human level error as an estimate or as a proxy for Bayes' error, but you think

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.960
that Bayes' error is close to 7.5%, then there's not that much headroom for reducing your

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.260
training error further down.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.880
You don't really want it to be that much better than 7.5% because you could achieve that only

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.780
by maybe starting to overfit the training set.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:36.140
And instead there's much more room for improvement in terms of taking this 2% gap and trying

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:42.080
to reduce that by using variance reduction techniques such as regularization, or maybe

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.840
getting more training data.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:50.400
So to give these things a couple of names, this is not widely used terminology, but I

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.700
found this useful terminology and useful way of thinking about it, which is what I call

00:04:55.700 --> 00:05:02.000
the difference between Bayes' error or approximation of Bayes' error and the training error to be

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.600
the avoidable bias.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.680
So what you want is to maybe keep improving your training performance until you get down

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:16.620
to Bayes' error, but you don't actually want to do better than Bayes' error.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.500
You can't actually do better than Bayes' error unless you're overfitting.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.920
And this, the difference between your training error and the def error, there's a measure

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.520
still of the variance problem of your algorithm.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:36.620
And the term avoidable bias acknowledges that there's some bias or some minimum level

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:43.180
of error that you just cannot get below, which is that if Bayes' error is 7.5%, you

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:47.180
don't actually want to get below that level of error.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.620
So rather than saying that if your training error is 8%, then the 8% is the measure of

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:52.620
the bias.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:06:01.500
In this example, you're saying that the avoidable bias is maybe 0.5% or 0.5% is a measure of

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.180
the avoidable bias, whereas 2% is a measure of the variance.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:12.300
And so there's much more room in reducing this 2% than in reducing this 0.5%.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.980
Whereas in contrast, in the example on the left, this 7% is a measure of the avoidable

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:24.340
bias, whereas 2% is a measure of how much variance you have.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:29.120
And so in this example on the left, there's much more potential in focusing on reducing

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.120
that avoidable bias.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:37.940
So in this example, understanding human level error, understanding your estimate of Bayes'

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.700
error already, causes you in different scenarios to focus on different tactics, whether bias

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:46.180
avoidance, tactics or variance avoidance tactics.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:50.500
There's quite a lot more nuance than how you factor in human level performance into how

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.580
you make decisions in choosing what to focus on.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.460
Let's in the next video go deeper into our understanding of what human level performance

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.260
really means.
